{:id "teammate-pull"
 :title "A teammate pushed first"
 :level :basic
 :instructions
 "Clone this repository — but you're not alone in it. Your teammate Pat is working too, and shared history means your pushes can collide with his. This one's a two-parter."

 :setup
 {:commits [{:name    :seed
             :branch  "main"
             :author  :curator
             :when    [:hours-before 2]
             :message "Set up the project\n"
             :files   {"welcome.txt" "Welcome to l2git!\n"}}]}

 ;; Pat lands in the gap between the learner's last fetch and their push:
 ;; the hook fires on the ref advertisement that starts their first push
 ;; attempt, so their own client rejects with "fetch first". (Triggering on
 ;; fetch instead would let clone-then-pull collect Pat's commit before any
 ;; work exists, bypassing the divergence entirely.) :once makes the hook
 ;; idempotent: it is skipped when the named commit already exists.
 :before-push
 {:branch  "main"
  :once    :pat-1
  :commits [{:name    :pat-1
             :branch  "main"
             :author  :pat
             :when    :now
             :message "Add my sprint notes\n"
             :files   {"welcome.txt"  "Welcome to l2git!\n"
                       "pat-notes.md" "- fix the flaky login test\n- review Sam's PR\n"}}]
  :event   "Pat pushed a commit to main"}

 :steps
 [{:id :collide
   :title "Make your move"
   :instructions
   "Create standup.md with a line about what you're working on, commit it on main, and push. Something is going to happen — watch git's output closely."
   :hints
   ["Work like you own the place: create the file, add it, commit it, push it."
    "echo \"- fixing the login flow\" > standup.md, then git add standup.md, git commit -m \"Add standup\", git push origin main"
    "Your push is going to be refused — that's the point, not a mistake. While you worked, the remote moved. Read what git prints: it tells you exactly what happened and what to do about it."]
   :checks [{:cond [:known :pat-1]
             :fail "Commit standup.md on main and push it — I'll be here."}]
   :pass "Pat pushed while you were working, and git refused your push. That's a collision, not a catastrophe — nothing is lost."}

  {:id :integrate
   :title "Integrate and push again"
   :instructions
   "Your history and Pat's have diverged. Get his commit (git pull, or git fetch then merge/rebase), make sure both your standup.md and his pat-notes.md survive, and push again. Whatever you do, don't force-push over him."
   :hints
   ["The rejection told you what to do: your view of the remote is stale, so refresh it first."
    "git pull --rebase origin main, then git push origin main. (Plain git pull works too — it merges instead of rebasing.)"
    "pull is fetch + integrate. With --rebase, your commit is replayed on top of Pat's, keeping history linear; without it, git makes a merge commit joining both lines. Either way both commits survive — force-push is the only move here that destroys work."]
   :checks [{:cond [:branch-exists "main"]
             :fail "I don't see a main branch yet."}
            {:cond [:reachable :pat-1 "main"]
             :fail "Pat's commit is missing from main — did you force-push over it? That's exactly how teammates lose work. Pat is pushing his notes again: pull, then push without force."}
            {:cond [:file-exists "main" "standup.md"]
             :fail "Pat's work made it, but I don't see your standup.md yet."}
            {:cond [:file-nonblank "main" "standup.md"]
             :fail "standup.md is there but empty — write a line about what you're working on."}
            {:cond [:file-exists "main" "pat-notes.md"]
             :fail "Pat's file (pat-notes.md) went missing during your integration. Merge or rebase again, keeping Pat's changes."}]
   :pass "Both your commit and Pat's live on main. You just survived your first diverged history — merge or rebase, either was right."}]

 ;; A force-push that clobbers Pat leaves his commit unreachable (a plain
 ;; fetch can't rescue it), so Pat notices and pushes his notes again on top
 ;; of whatever the learner left. Re-using :name :pat-1 rebinds the known
 ;; hash the :evaluate checks look for.
 :after-evaluate
 {:when    [:and [:failed]
                 [:known :pat-1]
                 [:branch-exists "main"]
                 [:not [:reachable :pat-1 "main"]]]
  :commits [{:name    :pat-1
             :branch  "main"
             :author  :pat
             :when    :now
             :message "Restore my sprint notes\n"
             :files   [:merge [:files-at "main"]
                       {"pat-notes.md" "- fix the flaky login test\n- review Sam's PR\n"}]}]
  :event   "Pat noticed his commit vanished and pushed his notes again — pull before continuing"}}
